  <body><div class="shell"><SiteHeader current="tutorials" /><main>
    <a class="back" href="/tutorials/">← Back to tutorials</a><div class="eyebrow">Tutorials / Tools / Drilling / Materials</div><h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="dek">Open a drill-bit case and the choices can look unnecessarily complicated. Some bits are long and spiral-shaped. Some are flat. Some look like tiny cutting cups. One looks like a staircase. Another barely resembles a conventional drill bit.</p>
    <p class="dek">Those shapes are not decoration. Geometry changes how a cutter starts, removes material, clears chips and controls the hole. The helpful question is not “what bit fits my drill?” It is “what material am I cutting, and what kind of hole do I need?”</p>
    <div class="byline">Published September 23, 2026 · Workshop Girl</div><ShareButton title={title} canonical={canonical} />
    <article>
      <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/tutorials/drill-bit-types/workshopgirl-drill-bit-types.webp" alt="WorkshopGirl comparing different types of drill bits and hole-making tools on a workshop bench" width="1672" height="941" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>Different shapes exist for a reason. The material and the hole you need should decide which bit comes out of the toolbox.</figcaption></figure>
      <aside class="rule-card" aria-labelledby="material-rule-title"><h2 id="material-rule-title">The WorkshopGirl rule</h2><p class="rule-phrase"><strong>Material first. Hole second. Bit third.</strong></p><p>Pick for the material, hole size and depth, finish you need, and the tool and application. A bit that fits the chuck is only compatible with the holder; it is not automatically right for the cut.</p></aside>

      <h2>Quick choice guide</h2><p>Start here, then read the section for the bit that seems closest. Exact suitability depends on bit design, material, tool, manufacturer guidance and application.</p>
      <div class="choice-grid" aria-label="Quick drill-bit choice guide">
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Twist bit</h3><p>Common general drilling when the specific bit suits the material.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Brad-point</h3><p>Accurate woodworking holes with a point that helps the bit start where marked.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Spade</h3><p>Fast, relatively large holes in wood when a rougher edge is acceptable.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Forstner</h3><p>Controlled larger woodworking holes and partial-depth recesses.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Masonry</h3><p>Masonry materials, with a bit and drill setup suited to the job.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Step bit</h3><p>Progressively sized holes in suitable relatively thin materials.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Countersink</h3><p>A conical recess lets a compatible countersunk screw head sit flush or recessed.</p></section>
        <section class="choice-card"><h3>Hole saw</h3><p>Large-diameter holes cut mainly around the perimeter.</p></section>
      </div>

      <h2>1. Twist drill bit</h2><p>The familiar twist bit has a pointed tip and spiral flutes running up a cylindrical body. The cutting edges at the tip shear material; the flutes provide space for chips to move back out of the hole.</p><p>“Twist bit” describes a shape, not one universal material specification. Bits are made with different base materials, heat treatments, coatings and point geometries. A suitable twist bit is useful for general drilling, but use one intended for the material and job. A bit designed for steel is not automatically the best choice for clean woodworking, and a wood bit is not a metal-drilling bit.</p>

      <h2>2. Brad-point wood bit</h2><p>A brad-point bit is made for woodworking. Its sharp center spur helps locate the hole; the outer spurs score the circumference before the inner cutting edges remove material. That combination helps the bit start at the mark and defines the edge of the hole.</p><p>A general twist bit can wander before its cutting lips settle, especially on a smooth surface. The brad-point’s center helps resist that wandering. It is useful when starting accurately and getting a clean, well-defined wood hole matters, but it is not automatically the best choice for every wood hole or every material.</p>

      <h2>3. Spade bit</h2><p>A spade bit is a flat paddle with a sharp center point and broad cutting edges. The point locates the cut while the edges remove wood quickly, making this style useful for relatively large holes where speed matters more than a furniture-grade finish.</p><p>The exit side can splinter as the point and cutting edges break through. When the layout allows, clamp a sacrificial backing board behind the work, or stop when the point just emerges and finish from the opposite face. Those methods can reduce breakout; they do not guarantee a perfect edge.</p>

      <h2>4. Forstner bit</h2><p>A Forstner bit uses a circular rim and internal cutting edges to define and remove material. Depending on the design, a center spur or other guidance feature helps locate the cutter. The rim guides the circumference, which is why the bit can make controlled larger holes and recesses in wood, including partial-depth work with a relatively flat-looking bottom.</p><p>That bottom is not mathematically identical across every bit: the center guidance and cutter geometry leave their own mark. The useful difference is control and a clean-sided recess, not a promise that every design leaves a perfectly flat floor.</p>
      <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/tutorials/drill-bit-types/workshopgirl-forstner-bit-wood.webp" alt="WorkshopGirl using a Forstner bit to create a partial-depth circular recess in a clamped wooden board" width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Forstner bit removes material very differently from an ordinary twist bit, making it useful for controlled larger woodworking holes and recesses.</figcaption></figure>
      <h3>Why a Forstner bit feels different</h3><p>A larger cutting diameter puts more edge into contact with the work. More edge engagement can mean more cutting load on the drill and more reaction at your hands. Secure workholding and controlled speed and feed become increasingly important as the cutter gets larger.</p><p>For a large Forstner bit or demanding cut, use equipment and a setup appropriate to the diameter and task. A drill press can be a better fit for some work; not every Forstner operation belongs on a handheld cordless drill.</p>

      <h2>5. Masonry bit</h2><p>A masonry bit has a spiral body but a specialized cutting tip that is visibly different from the sharpened point of an ordinary metal twist bit. Many common masonry bits use a carbide-based or carbide-tipped cutting edge to work on compatible masonry materials.</p><p>Match the bit and setup to the material. Whether rotation-only or hammer action is appropriate depends on the bit, material, tool and manufacturer instructions. Do not select hammer mode automatically just because the wall is masonry; fragile tile, brittle material or a particular bit may call for a different approach.</p>

      <h2>6. Step drill bit</h2><p>A step bit has a cone-shaped body with a series of cutting steps. Each step corresponds to a progressively larger diameter. It can make or enlarge several hole sizes with one cutter, which is especially useful in suitable relatively thin sheet materials.</p><p>Use only within the bit’s intended material and thickness range. A step bit is not a universal replacement for a conventional drill bit in thick stock or every hole-making job.</p>

      <h2>7. Countersink bit</h2><p>A countersink cutter makes a conical recess around an existing hole so a compatible countersunk screw head can sit flush with or below the surface. The basic sequence is:</p><div class="rule-card"><p><strong>Pilot or clearance hole, as required → countersink recess → compatible screw head seats in the recess.</strong></p></div><p>Fastener standards use different head geometries and angles, so do not assume one countersink angle fits every screw. Match the cutter to the actual fastener and follow the relevant specification.</p>

      <h2>8. Hole saw</h2><p>A hole saw is a toothed cylindrical cup on an arbor. Where its design includes one, a pilot bit helps start and guide the cut. The teeth remove a ring of material around the circumference, leaving a central plug rather than turning the whole hole into chips.</p><p>Hole saws are useful for larger-diameter holes when the cutter is made for the material and application. Speed, secure workholding and the tool’s capacity matter. If the toothed cup catches, a large cutter can produce substantial reaction torque—support the tool, keep a firm grip and use any required side handle or setup from the tool maker.</p>

      <h2>Start with the material</h2><p>The same hole size can call for different tools in different materials. The image below is a reminder to choose for the cut; its examples are not universal prescriptions.</p>
      <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/tutorials/drill-bit-types/workshopgirl-drill-bits-wood-metal-masonry.webp" alt="WorkshopGirl comparing drill bits for wood, metal, and masonry materials" width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Start with the material. A wood bit, metal-drilling bit and masonry bit may all make holes, but their cutting geometry is designed around different jobs.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="material-grid">
        <section class="material-card"><h3>Wood</h3><ul><li>Brad-point for a controlled start</li><li>Suitable twist bit for general holes</li><li>Spade for faster large holes</li><li>Forstner for controlled holes and recesses</li><li>Hole saw for compatible large diameters</li></ul></section>
        <section class="material-card"><h3>Metal</h3><ul><li>A twist drill designed for the specific metal and thickness</li><li>Step bit for suitable sheet applications</li><li>Compatible hole saw for larger openings</li></ul></section>
        <section class="material-card"><h3>Masonry</h3><ul><li>A masonry-specific bit intended for the material</li><li>A drill and mode suited to that bit and application</li></ul></section>
      </div><p>There is no universal “best drill bit.” Ask: <strong>best bit for what material and what hole?</strong></p>

      <h2>Bit material and coatings</h2><p>Shape is only part of the story. Base material, heat treatment, coating, cutting geometry, manufacturing quality and intended work material all affect how a bit performs.</p><ul><li><strong>High-speed steel (HSS):</strong> a common family for general metal-drilling bits, with designs that vary by material and duty.</li><li><strong>Cobalt-alloy HSS:</strong> cobalt is part of the alloy rather than just a surface color; some grades are chosen for particular harder or heat-sensitive drilling applications.</li><li><strong>Carbide or carbide-tipped cutters:</strong> used in specific bit families and applications, including many masonry bits.</li><li><strong>Coated bits:</strong> surface treatments vary and should be identified by the maker’s specification.</li></ul><p>A gold finish alone does not prove a bit is titanium-coated or tell you what it is suited to. Check the maker’s actual material and application details.</p>

      <h2>Round shank vs hex shank</h2><p>Many conventional drill bits have a round shank intended for a drill chuck. Some bits and accessories have a 1/4-inch hex shank for compatible quick-change holders. Shank shape tells you about the holder, not necessarily whether the cutting end suits your material.</p><p>Compatibility and suitability are separate questions: a bit can fit an impact driver and still not be appropriate for impact loading. For more on tool holders and when each tool makes sense, read <a href="/tutorials/cordless-drill-vs-impact-driver">Cordless Drill vs Impact Driver: What’s the Difference?</a></p>

      <h2>How to choose the right bit</h2><ol class="decision-list"><li><strong>What material?</strong> Identify wood, metal, masonry or another material—and any coating, surface layer or reinforcement.</li><li><strong>What hole?</strong> Is it a small through-hole, pilot hole, large opening, partial-depth recess, countersunk screw seat or sheet-material hole?</li><li><strong>What finish matters?</strong> A rough utility opening and a clean visible woodworking hole do not ask the cutter to do the same job.</li><li><strong>What tool are you using?</strong> Consider the drill/driver, drill press, hammer drill or compatible impact-driver accessory, plus its limits.</li><li><strong>What does the bit maker specify?</strong> Check intended material, diameter, depth, speed guidance and any tool or mode requirements.</li></ol>

      <h2>Size matters</h2><p>A larger diameter generally means more cutting edge engaged and more material being removed. That can affect speed, feed pressure, heat, tool load, reaction torque and how firmly the work must be secured.</p><p>There is no one RPM chart that covers every bit. The right speed depends on cutter type and diameter, material, tool and cutting conditions. Follow the bit and tool makers’ guidance; if the cutter binds, overheats or behaves unexpectedly, stop and reassess.</p>

      <h2>Common beginner mistakes</h2><ul><li>Choosing a bit because it physically fits the chuck</li><li>Using a bit unsuited to the material</li><li>Continuing with a dull, bent or damaged bit</li><li>Holding loose work by hand instead of securing it</li><li>Forcing the drill or using excessive speed</li><li>Starting out of alignment or ignoring chip evacuation</li><li>Letting heat build up rather than following material-specific guidance</li><li>Using an impact driver with accessories not suited to impact loading</li><li>Assuming every gold-colored bit has the same material or coating</li><li>Treating masonry, metal and wood drilling as identical operations</li><li>Forgetting that a spade bit may break out at the back of wood</li><li>Using a large cutter without considering reaction torque and workholding</li></ul>

      <h2>Work safely</h2><ul><li>Wear task-appropriate eye protection and secure the workpiece.</li><li>Keep loose hair and clothing controlled, and hands clear of rotating cutters.</li><li>Inspect and install the bit correctly; stop the tool before adjusting it.</li><li>Consider reaction torque with larger cutters and use the setup or handle the tool maker specifies.</li><li>Follow both tool and bit manufacturer guidance.</li></ul><p>Avoid loose gloves around exposed rotating cutters where they could catch and pull a hand into the tool.</p>

      <aside class="quick-rule" aria-labelledby="quick-rule-title"><h2 id="quick-rule-title">WorkshopGirl Quick Rule</h2><p class="rule-phrase"><strong>Material first. Hole second. Bit third.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Wood + clean accurate hole?</strong> Consider a woodworking-specific bit.</li><li><strong>Large wood hole?</strong> A spade, Forstner or hole saw may fit, depending on the result you need.</li><li><strong>Metal?</strong> Choose a bit intended for that metal and application.</li><li><strong>Masonry?</strong> Use a masonry-specific cutter and appropriate drill setup.</li><li><strong>Big hole?</strong> Do not automatically reach for a giant twist bit.</li></ul><p><strong>The bit is part of the cutting system — not just something that fits in the chuck.</strong></p></aside>

      <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><h2 id="faq-title">Drill Bit Types FAQ</h2>
        <h3>What drill bit should a beginner use?</h3><p>It depends on the material and hole. For a small hole in wood, a suitable twist or brad-point bit may make sense; for masonry, use a masonry bit and appropriate setup. Check the bit maker’s intended use.</p>
        <h3>Can I use the same drill bit for wood and metal?</h3><p>Some suitable twist drills can work in certain wood and metal applications, but not every twist bit is for every material. A wood-specific geometry may start and finish better in wood; select a metal bit for the actual metal and thickness.</p>
        <h3>What is the difference between a twist bit and a brad-point bit?</h3><p>A twist bit uses a conventional pointed tip and spiral flutes for chip removal. A brad-point is designed for wood and adds a locating spur and outer scoring spurs to help start and define the hole.</p>
        <h3>What is a Forstner bit used for?</h3><p>It makes controlled larger holes and recesses in wood. Depending on its design and setup, it can make a partial-depth recess with a relatively flat-looking bottom.</p>
        <h3>What is the difference between a spade bit and a Forstner bit?</h3><p>A spade bit is a flat paddle that removes wood quickly, often with a rougher exit edge. A Forstner bit cuts around a rim and is commonly chosen for more controlled larger holes or recesses.</p>
        <h3>What drill bit should I use for concrete?</h3><p>Choose a masonry bit rated for the material and match it to an appropriate drill and mode. Follow the tool and bit instructions; hammer action is not right for every material or cutter.</p>
        <h3>What is a step drill bit used for?</h3><p>It makes progressively larger holes in suitable relatively thin material, often sheet material. Check the specific bit’s capacity and application.</p>
        <h3>What is a countersink bit used for?</h3><p>It cuts a conical recess around a hole so a compatible countersunk screw head can sit flush or recessed. Match the recess to the actual fastener geometry.</p>
        <h3>When should I use a hole saw?</h3><p>Use a compatible hole saw when you need a larger-diameter opening and the material, cutter, tool and setup are suited to the job.</p>
        <h3>Can I use normal drill bits in an impact driver?</h3><p>Only if the shank fits a compatible holder or adapter—and fitting is not enough. The accessory must also be suitable for impact loading and the material.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="related" aria-labelledby="related-title"><h2 id="related-title">Keep Learning</h2><div class="related-grid">
        <a class="related-card" href="/tutorials/how-to-use-a-cordless-drill/"><strong>How to Use a Cordless Drill: A Beginner’s Guide</strong><span>Learn drill controls, bit selection, pilot holes, clutch settings and safe technique.</span></a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/tutorials/cordless-drill-vs-impact-driver"><strong>Cordless Drill vs Impact Driver: What’s the Difference?</strong><span>Compare their tool holders, mechanisms and best-fit jobs.</span></a>
      </div></section>
      <div class="closing"><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>A drill bit is a cutter with a particular geometry, not a universal pointed accessory. Start with the material and the hole you need, choose the bit that suits both, secure the work and follow the maker’s guidance. Once you recognize why the shapes differ, that crowded bit case gets a lot less mysterious.</p></div>
    </article>
